WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.260
Coca

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.239
Cola was going to cure morphine

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:06.049
addiction.

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:07.459
Bubble wrap was supposed to be a

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:10.159
trendy new textured wallpaper

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:11.269
and YouTube was going to be the

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:13.669
premiere video dating app.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:15.409
More often, failed products don't

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:17.749
lead to accidental successes,

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:18.739
but they're nevertheless an

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.019
essential feature of capitalism.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.489
It was Karl Marx who set out to

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:23.749
bring production under conscious

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.759
control, correcting for the anarchic

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.679
forces of the free market.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.719
But it turned out that anarchy,

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.579
risk taking and the freedom to try

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.559
out wild ideas is the

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.209
only process that leads to

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.099
successful innovation

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.259
because nobody can predict consumer

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:40.779
tastes.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.939
A doll that grows breasts, a hula

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:44.246
chair for office workers, a virtual

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.379
reality glove that hit the market in

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.380
1989, these

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:52.269
and other commercial flops

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:53.799
are on display at the Museum of

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:55.180
Failure in Washington.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.609
We are very much trying to have fun

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:04.749
with this exhibit.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:06.159
So people like to make fun of the

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.829
fact that we are on the site of a

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.479
failed gem.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:11.799
Joanna Guttman is one of the

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.599
organizers of the exhibit.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.849
It's the brainchild of Dr. Samuel

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.709
West. He is the curator

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.599
of the show. And he realized

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.249
that the most successful companies

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.079
were those that had a high

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.429
failure rate.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.049
So he started to explore this

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:27.819
concept of failure because everyone

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.709
loves to talk about success.

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.419
No one likes to talk about failure

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.099
when in fact failure is a much

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.679
better teacher than successes.

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:37.299
The subway is actually a perfect

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.829
example, because some people will

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:39.969
say, Well, why would you call the

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:41.259
subway a failure?

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.479
The expectation with the Segway

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.789
was that entire city infrastructures

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.739
would be built around Segways

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.659
and people would use it as a mode

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.039
of transportation.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.719
But today, we really use subways.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.249
I mean, we all think of that movie

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.199
mall cop ride and

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.639
those tourist tours.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:01:59.169
That's essentially where they've

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:00.279
been relegated to.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:04.149
Meet new growing up skipper.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:06.369
He's two dolls in one

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:08.079
because when you turn her arm, you

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:09.668
can make her change instantly from a

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:11.439
little girl to a tall, slender

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.329
teenage doll, which is something you

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:14.439
can't do.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.629
It's such a like, creepy thing

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:18.909
that she, like, develops breasts and

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:20.889
gets two dolls in one.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:22.329
There could not have been any women

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:23.259
at that table. Right.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.489
I think that and the pregnant Barbie

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:25.709
came out around the same time.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:27.099
So it was like, who was thinking

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.259
about this?

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:29.889
Know, it's bizarre.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.569
So there definitely is a tone

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:33.759
deafness sometimes in

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:34.449
marketing.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:35.739
There are like quite a few products

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:36.999
in here that are like, quote

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.439
unquote, before their time.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.819
You have something very similar that

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:41.139
it looks like VR goggles, but it's

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.729
obviously obviously not virtual

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:43.749
reality. Right?

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:44.529
Right. Yeah.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:45.729
Sometimes there are marketing

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.649
reasons why something is marketed

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.479
poorly and that that leads to

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:50.480
the product's failure.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:52.869
The discreet sanitary solution.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:54.219
For your urgent relief.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:55.869
Guys, how many times has this

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:56.859
happened to you?

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.509
Come on. You know you can't hold it

00:02:58.510 --> 00:02:59.529
in that long.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:00.459
Don't worry.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.549
I've got the perfect gift.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.549
It comes with a channel and a

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.379
piercing. Sometimes it's

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:06.789
just a timing issue.

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:08.229
But those things kind of pave the

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.059
way for how see our products.

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:10.839
Exactly.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.729
So we actually have Google Glass,

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:14.829
which is great, because

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:16.509
nobody would consider Google a

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.159
failure as a company, even though

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.779
not every product sort of hit the

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:21.519
mark. But people are now saying,

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.229
number one, a new Google Glass is

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:24.009
coming out.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:25.899
We actually had a poster of it at

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:27.759
the New York show where somebody

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:28.689
said, stay tuned.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:30.189
They work at Google and a new one is

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:31.299
coming out.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:32.319
And it was anonymous.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.320
So

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:35.919
we have some big issues to deal

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:37.479
with. If we're only going to go with

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.099
safe choices or things that we know

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:40.509
that absolutely will work, we

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:41.499
probably are going to end up with

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.389
nothing. So we want to solve

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:44.619
big problems. We're going to have to

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.989
try. And sometimes the technology

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.419
like with the Nintendo glove,

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.729
which was very...

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:53.109
People were super excited about it

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:54.399
when it came out, but it had all

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:55.689
these buttons that really didn't do

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:57.759
anything but the same technology

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:59.409
was used for the Wii, which was a

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:00.999
very big commercial success.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:02.649
So sometimes the same technology is

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:04.629
used also for a

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:05.829
following product.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:07.389
But tech companies in general are

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:09.549
really good at sort of learning

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:10.869
from their mistakes.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:12.579
And I don't want to really even call

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:14.109
it mistakes, but learning from

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:15.969
through trial and error and taking

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:16.970
in that feedback.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.139
We are trying to provoke a

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:22.209
conversation about failure,

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:24.309
about success, about innovation.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:25.959
The museum also has a section

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.179
devoted to Elon Musk, who

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:29.919
love him or hate him, from PayPal to

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:31.929
SpaceX, from Tesla to StarLink,

00:04:31.930 --> 00:04:33.909
is one of the most successful

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:36.099
entrepreneurs of our time.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.079
But that has a lot to do with his

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.869
willingness to take wild risks.

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.819
Failure must be an option if

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:43.809
failure is not an option.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:44.739
There are a lot of extremely

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:47.409
conservative choices, and

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:49.419
you may get something even

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:50.420
worse, like in aviation, things

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:52.629
may go backwards.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.009
He's just really pushing that

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:55.899
envelope all the time, and he makes

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.969
us think he's provocative, which

00:04:57.970 --> 00:04:58.989
we are trying to be.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:02.289
So we definitely want to encourage

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:04.479
people to take meaningful risks.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.309
I also think that we live in a time

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:07.989
where social media amplifies

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:10.119
everything, and I think young people

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:11.979
have this real fear of

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:13.779
failing because it's going to follow

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.669
me forever or it's going to seem

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.529
maybe bigger than it is because it

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:19.449
is on social media.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:21.579
So we're trying to really sort of

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:23.199
make people more comfortable with

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.759
this idea that not everything is

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.289
going to work out the way we planned

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:27.759
or the way we had wanted.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.529
If we're always going to be safe and

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:31.719
take the safe route so that

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:33.669
we almost can guarantee

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:35.499
success, then we're not

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.329
going to solve big problems

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.729
and address situations

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.859
because we're not taking any risks

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:43.179
and then we don't innovate and we

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:44.169
don't progress.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:46.179
There are catastrophic failures

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.249
happening all around us

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.109
and we're so much richer because of

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:51.110
it.

